@page "/"

<PageTitle>Child Care Licensing and Compliance</PageTitle>

<div class="hero">
    <h1>Licensing and compliance for child care centres</h1>
    <p class="lead">
        A demonstration of how a centre is licensed and monitored: an operator applies, the
        requested capacity is checked against staffing-ratio and floor-area rules, inspectors
        record findings, ministry staff track compliance risk, and the public can look up any
        centre on the register.
    </p>
</div>

<section aria-labelledby="audiences-heading">
    <h2 id="audiences-heading">Choose where to start</h2>

    <div class="card-grid">
        <article class="entry-card">
            <p class="audience audience-operator">Operator view</p>
            <h3><a href="/facilities">Apply to license a centre</a></h3>
            <p>
                Review the rooms in your centre, run the capacity check, and submit an
                application once every room passes.
            </p>
            <p class="entry-hint">Start with Sunshine Early Learning Centre, which has a draft application.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="entry-card">
            <p class="audience audience-ministry">Ministry staff view</p>
            <h3><a href="/reports">Monitor compliance</a></h3>
            <p>
                Rank centres by risk: overdue findings, lapsed licences, upcoming expiries,
                and centres that have gone a year without an inspection.
            </p>
            <p class="entry-hint">Both reports are produced by SQL Server stored procedures.</p>
        </article>

        <article class="entry-card">
            <p class="audience audience-public">Public view</p>
            <h3><a href="/registry">Find a licensed centre</a></h3>
            <p>
                Search the register by city or centre name to see licence status, expiry date,
                and inspection history. No account needed.
            </p>
            <p class="entry-hint">Operator contact details and reviewer notes are never shown here.</p>
        </article>
    </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="rules-heading">
    <h2 id="rules-heading">The rules being applied</h2>
    <p>
        Each room is checked against three limits and the lowest one decides the licensed
        capacity. These are simplified from the age-group requirements under the
        <em>Child Care and Early Years Act</em>.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
            <caption class="visually-hidden">Capacity rules by age group</caption>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th scope="col">Age group</th>
                    <th scope="col">Staff to children</th>
                    <th scope="col">Maximum group size</th>
                    <th scope="col">Floor area per child</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Infant</th><td>1 : 3</td><td>12</td><td>2.5 m&sup2;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Toddler</th><td>1 : 5</td><td>15</td><td>2.5 m&sup2;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Preschool</th><td>1 : 8</td><td>16</td><td>2.8 m&sup2;</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">School age</th><td>1 : 15</td><td>26</td><td>2.8 m&sup2;</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="build-heading">
    <h2 id="build-heading">How it is built</h2>
    <ul class="plain-list">
        <li>ASP.NET Core Web API and Blazor Server on .NET 10, in a layered solution.</li>
        <li>The capacity rules engine sits in the domain layer with no infrastructure dependencies.</li>
        <li>Entity Framework Core handles transactional work; Dapper and stored procedures handle reporting.</li>
        <li>A background worker expires licences, issues renewal notices, and escalates overdue findings.</li>
    </ul>
</section>
